  <section class="flu-section flu-history">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">A little history</span>
      <h2 class="disp">The pandemic hiding behind a war.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flu-history-grid">
      <article>
        <h3 class="disp">A war-time secret.</h3>
        <p>The leading theory places the first major outbreak at Camp Funston, an army base in Haskell County, Kansas, in March 1918 — though China and northern France have also been proposed. What's certain is that wartime troop movements spread it fast, and wartime censorship in the US, UK, France, and Germany kept it quiet to protect morale. Spain, neutral in the war, reported freely — including its own king's illness — and gave the pandemic a name it never really earned.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3 class="disp">Then it came back for blood.</h3>
        <p>A mild spring wave gave little warning of what followed. By fall, the virus had mutated into something far deadlier, killing in three waves through the winter of 1919. It broke the usual pattern, too — striking healthy adults in their twenties and thirties hardest, possibly because their stronger immune systems overreacted, turning the body's own defense into the danger.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <div class="flu-timeline-outer">
      <button type="button" class="tl-arrow prev" id="tlPrev" aria-label="Previous milestone">‹</button>
      <div class="flu-timeline" id="fluTimeline" aria-label="1918 influenza milestones" tabindex="0">
        <div class="tl-sp" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">Mar 1918</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">Camp Funston</b><p>An army cook reports to the infirmary with flu-like symptoms; by lunch, over a hundred soldiers have followed him.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">Spring 1918</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">The mild wave</b><p>The virus spreads with troop movements across the U.S. and Europe, largely unnoticed amid war news, with relatively low fatality.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">Summer 1918</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">Named by accident</b><p>Spain's uncensored press freely reports the outbreak, including the king's illness — and the world starts calling it the "Spanish flu."</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev sev"><div class="yr">Aug 1918</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">It mutates</b><p>Nearly simultaneous outbreaks in Freetown, Brest, and Boston mark the start of a far deadlier second wave.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev sev"><div class="yr">28 Sept 1918</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">Philadelphia's parade</b><p>The city holds a 200,000-person Liberty Loan parade against doctors' advice. The flu explodes within days.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev sev"><div class="yr">Oct 1918</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">The deadliest month</b><p>Roughly 195,000 Americans die in October alone — the worst single month in U.S. history, for any cause.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev ok"><div class="yr">Oct 1918</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">St. Louis's gamble</b><p>Acting within two days of its first cases, the city closes schools, theaters, and gatherings — and records a peak death rate less than half Philadelphia's.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">11 Nov 1918</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">The war ends, the dying continues</b><p>Armistice Day crowds worldwide add fresh fuel to the pandemic even as World War I ends.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">Winter 1919</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">The third wave</b><p>A smaller resurgence hits into spring, then fades as population immunity builds.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">1919</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">Wilson falls ill</b><p>U.S. President Woodrow Wilson is struck by the flu during the Paris peace talks; some historians argue it affected his judgment on the Treaty of Versailles.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">1920</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">It disappears</b><p>The pandemic ends abruptly — not through medicine, since no vaccine or antiviral existed, but through herd immunity and the virus itself drifting toward milder seasonal strains.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">1933</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">The virus, isolated</b><p>Influenza A is isolated in a lab for the first time — more than a decade after the pandemic that made its study urgent.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev ok"><div class="yr">1997–2005</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">Resurrected from permafrost</b><p>Scientists recover fragments of the 1918 virus from a victim preserved in Alaskan permafrost and fully reconstruct its genome by 2005 — the first pandemic pathogen ever rebuilt from history.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tl-ev"><div class="yr">2009</div><div class="body"><b class="disp">A descendant returns</b><p>The 2009 "swine flu" pandemic is caused by a descendant of the same viral lineage — far milder, thanks to partial population immunity.</p></div></div>
