  <section class="flu-section flu-compare-section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">The cool diagram</span>
      <h2 class="disp">The fourteen-day gap.</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="flu-sub">Same virus, same country, same autumn. Philadelphia and St. Louis recorded their first cases within three weeks of each other in the fall of 1918. The only real variable between them was how many days each city waited to act — and it shows up directly in the death toll.</p>
    <div class="flu-compare-grid" id="fluCompare">
      <article class="flu-compare-card flu-compare-bad">
        <span class="eyebrow">Waited</span>
        <h3 class="disp">Philadelphia</h3>
        <ul class="flu-compare-log">
          <li><b>Sept 18</b> — first cases confirmed among sailors at the Navy Yard.</li>
          <li><b>Sept 28</b> — a 200,000-person Liberty Loan parade proceeds as planned.</li>
          <li><b>Oct 3</b> — schools, churches, and theaters finally ordered closed, five days after the parade, with hospitals already overwhelmed.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="flu-compare-stat"><span class="n">748</span><span class="k">excess deaths per 100,000 — among the highest of any U.S. city</span></div>
      </article>
      <article class="flu-compare-card flu-compare-good">
        <span class="eyebrow">Acted</span>
        <h3 class="disp">St. Louis</h3>
        <ul class="flu-compare-log">
          <li><b>Oct 5</b> — first cases confirmed in the city.</li>
          <li><b>Oct 7</b> — schools, theaters, and public gatherings banned, two days later.</li>
          <li><b>Result</b> — the epidemic curve stayed flat enough that hospitals never ran out of beds.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="flu-compare-stat"><span class="n">358</span><span class="k">excess deaths per 100,000 — less than half Philadelphia's</span></div>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="flu-ring-foot">Excess-death figures are historical estimates from public-health research comparing 1918 U.S. cities' interventions and outcomes.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="flu-section flu-spotlight">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Exhumed twice · Brevig Mission, Alaska</span>
      <h2 class="disp">A village's tragedy, exhumed twice.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flu-spotlight-grid">
      <article class="flu-spotlight-lead">
        <p>In November 1918, the flu reached the small Alaska Native village of Brevig Mission by mail sled. Within five days, roughly 90% of its adult population was dead — about 72 of its 80 or so residents — leaving a generation of orphaned children. The dead were buried together in permafrost, in a mass grave that would, decades later, hold the key to understanding what had killed them.</p>
        <p>In 1951, pathologist Johan Hultin traveled to the site hoping to recover the virus from frozen tissue; the samples failed. In 1997, with the village council's permission, he returned and succeeded, sending preserved lung tissue to Jeffery Taubenberger's team at the Armed Forces Institute of Pathology. By 2005, they had fully reconstructed the 1918 virus's genome — the first time scientists had ever rebuilt an extinct pandemic pathogen, and a moment that immediately raised its own questions about who should be allowed to do that.</p>
      </article>
      <aside class="flu-spotlight-facts">
        <div class="flu-fact"><span class="n">~90%</span><span class="k">of Brevig Mission's adult population died within five days, November 1918</span></div>
        <div class="flu-fact"><span class="n">1951</span><span class="k">Hultin's first, failed attempt to recover the virus from the permafrost</span></div>
        <div class="flu-fact"><span class="n">1997</span><span class="k">his second attempt succeeds, with the village council's permission</span></div>
        <div class="flu-fact flu-fact-hope"><span class="n">2005</span><span class="k">the 1918 virus's genome is fully reconstructed — a scientific first</span></div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="flu-section flu-close">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">The reckoning</span>
      <h2 class="disp">The deadliest pandemic in modern history. No vaccine. No cure.</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="flu-close-line">The 1918 pandemic killed more people than the war it rode in on, using nothing but distance, closed doors, and time — because no vaccine or antiviral existed yet. Philadelphia and St. Louis had access to exactly the same tools; the only difference was <b>speed</b>. That lesson is still the cheapest one in public health, and still the hardest to act on before it looks necessary.</p>
